      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To determine the association between the change in weight status and newly developed snoring in children."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "This was a prospective cohort study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Structured interviews were conducted at participants' homes, and physical examinations were performed at The-pa Community Hospital in Southern Thailand."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Cohort subjects (n = 1076) were recruited from children born over a 1-year period between November 2000 and October 2001 in The-pa District of Songkhla Province, Southern Thailand. Data collection was performed when the child was 5 and 8.5 years old."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "The child's snoring, weight, height and tonsillar size were examined. The change in weight status was categorised into four groups: not obese, became not obese, became obese and persistently obese."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Of 1076, 983 subjects (91.4%) completed both surveys. The overall prevalence of habitual snoring decreased from 13% at age 5 to 10.9% at age 8.5 years. Of the 855 children who reported not snoring in the first survey, 61 (7.1%) had become habitual snorers. After adjusting for tonsillar size, children who had become obese or were persistently obese had a significant association with becoming a new snorer with an odds ratio of 5.8 (Confidence interval, CI, 1.3-25.8) and 11.7 (CI, 2.5-54.9), respectively."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Children with newly developed obesity at 8.5 years old were strongly associated with new snoring occurrence, which is similar to the findings of long-term follow-up studies in adults. Our findings support the idea that the patterns of changes in weight status and snoring development in adults begin as early as the primary school years."
          }
        ]
      },
